# LLM Integration for Spell Generation

Defines patterns for integrating LLMs in Spellwright to translate natural language into structured spell JSON.

## Purpose

Obtain reliable structured JSON from natural language input with resilience, validation, and non-blocking UX.

## Scope

**Applies To:**

- Client to LLM API communication
- Prompt construction
- Response validation
- Error handling
- Logging and monitoring

## Communication Patterns

**DO:**

- Use asynchronous communication (`async/await`)
- Never block the main thread
- Implement timeout for all requests
- Use retry with exponential backoff
- Handle network failures gracefully
- Show loading indicators during requests

**DON'T:**

- Block UI during requests
- Use synchronous API calls
- Skip timeout configuration
- Retry indefinitely
- Hide loading state from user

## Three-Layer Validation

**Layer 1: API Function Calling**

- Use OpenAI Function Calling or equivalent
- Define strict JSON schema at API level
- Enforce type constraints
- Validate required fields

**Layer 2: Few-Shot Prompting**

- Include 2-3 examples per spell type
- Show correct JSON structure
- Demonstrate edge cases
- Keep examples current and tested
- Update examples when schema changes

**Layer 3: Client-Side Validation**

- Validate against complete JSON schema
- Check numeric ranges
- Verify enum values
- Validate modifier compatibility
- Enforce business rules
- Reject invalid combinations

**DO:**

- Implement all three layers
- Fail fast with clear messages
- Log validation failures
- Return actionable error messages

**DON'T:**

- Rely only on prompting
- Skip client-side validation
- Accept invalid JSON
- Process unvalidated data

## Error Handling

**Error Categories:**

**Network Errors:**

- Timeout, connection failure, DNS issues
- Action: Retry with backoff, show user-friendly message

**API Errors:**

- Rate limit, authentication, server errors
- Action: Log error, show specific message, don't retry auth errors

**Validation Errors:**

- Schema mismatch, invalid ranges, incompatible modifiers
- Action: Log details, show field-specific errors, allow retry

**Semantic Errors:**

- Misunderstood intent, incomplete spell
- Action: Show clarification prompt, suggest corrections

**DO:**

- Categorize errors correctly
- Show actionable messages to user
- Log errors with context
- Provide retry options when appropriate
- Include error codes for debugging

**DON'T:**

- Show technical stack traces to users
- Hide errors silently
- Retry authentication errors
- Use generic error messages

## Configuration Management

**Externalize:**

- API endpoint URL
- Model name/version
- Timeout values (request, retry)
- Max retry attempts
- Backoff multiplier
- API keys/credentials

**DO:**

- Use environment variables
- Use configuration files
- Support multiple environments (dev, staging, prod)
- Validate configuration on startup
- Document all config options

**DON'T:**

- Hardcode API keys in source
- Commit credentials to version control
- Use same config for all environments
- Skip config validation

## Logging and Monitoring

**Log Events:**

- Request initiated (timestamp, user input length)
- Prompt constructed (size, model, temperature)
- API called (endpoint, timeout)
- Response received (status, size, latency)
- Validation result (pass/fail, errors)
- Final outcome (success/failure)

**DO:**

- Log essential metadata
- Include correlation IDs
- Measure latency
- Track validation failures
- Monitor API usage
- Alert on error rate spikes

**DON'T:**

- Log sensitive user data
- Log API keys
- Log full prompt content (PII risk)
- Skip error logging
- Ignore performance metrics

## Prompt Construction

**DO:**

- Use system message for role and constraints
- Include JSON schema in prompt
- Add few-shot examples
- Specify output format clearly
- Set appropriate temperature (0.3-0.7)
- Limit max tokens to reasonable value

**DON'T:**

- Use overly long prompts
- Include outdated examples
- Skip schema definition
- Use high temperature (>0.8)
- Allow unlimited token generation

## Retry Strategy

**Exponential Backoff:**

- Initial delay: 1 second
- Multiplier: 2x
- Max attempts: 3
- Max delay: 10 seconds

**Retry Conditions:**

- Network timeout
- 5xx server errors
- Rate limit (429) with Retry-After header

**Don't Retry:**

- 4xx client errors (except 429)
- Authentication failures (401, 403)
- Validation errors

## Checklist

Before deploying LLM integration:

- [ ] Async requests with timeout
- [ ] Function Calling configured
- [ ] Few-shot examples current and tested
- [ ] Client-side validation implemented
- [ ] Error handling by category
- [ ] Retry with exponential backoff
- [ ] Configuration externalized
- [ ] API keys secured
- [ ] Logging enabled (no sensitive data)
- [ ] Loading indicators in UI
- [ ] User-friendly error messages

## Integration

This standard works with:

- **JSON Schema** (`concept/json-schema.mdc`)
